summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Johnson is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems.

The patient reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately, primarily in her elbows, and she wanted to talk to me about that. She has been sitting at the computer at her desk more at the office, and she is not sure if it is being stagnant in that position all day that has increased flare-ups. She is right-handed, and she types all day versus writing, so it flares up in both elbows. She states that her right elbow bothers her more than the other. She denies any other joint pain. She also reports that she occasionally feels a little bit of numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time, and she tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation. She has had a history of gout, and the last episode was about 3 months ago, and she had some inflammation of her toe. She states that the medication I gave her has controlled it, and she has not seen a flare-up since. She also has psoriasis, and she states that it has been under control as well.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain and stiffness. Denies left elbow pain.
Skin: Reports psoriasis.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Vitals are normal.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
CV: No cervical lymphadenopathy. Regular rate and rhythm.
MSK: Examination of the right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon with tenderness and effusion. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. Examination of the left elbow: No edema or inflammation. Examination of the abdomen: Soft and nontender. No lower extremity edema.

RESULTS

Autoimmune profile: Unremarkable.
ESR: Unremarkable.
Uric acid: Unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT

Gout.
- Chronic.
- Stable.

Osteoarthritis.
- Right elbow.
- X-ray of the right elbow ordered.

Psoriasis.
- Scalp.
- Steroid cream is continued.

PLAN

From a gout standpoint, everything is stable. Continue colchicine 0.6 mg twice daily as needed for flare-ups of gout. For her osteoarthritis, I would like to order an x-ray of her right elbow. If the x-ray shows any issues, we can refer her for physical therapy. From a psoriasis standpoint, everything is stable. We will continue her on the steroid cream for her scalp.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue taking your medications as prescribed. Follow up in one week to discuss the results of the x-ray.

HOSPITAL NOTES

The patient's right elbow x-ray is reviewed and revealed no significant findings. }}<|eot_id|>
