summary is: {{ CC:

Back pain.

HPI:

Ms. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and was involved in a surprise. She states she was behind other people and when they jumped out, she fell down the stairs and hit her back on the railing. She denies any pain down her legs. She states that her back hurts more when she is lying flat and with any twisting motion. She denies any back problems prior to this except for a lumbar fusion.

EXAM

Examination of the back is unremarkable for muscle spasms or tenderness to palpation. She has full range of motion in the back and extremities. No pain with extension, flexion, rotation or lateral bending. No pain with straight leg raise bilaterally. No paresthesias, weakness, or numbness. No pain with palpation of the paraspinal muscles.

RESULTS

X-rays of the lumbar spine obtained today are unremarkable for any fractures or dislocations. It does show a fusion at T4.

IMPRESSION

Based on the history and physical examination, the patient likely has a lumbar strain. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine. The patient has elected to follow up with outpatient MRI.

PLAN

At this point, I have recommended that she continue taking ibuprofen 800 mg three times a day with food. I have also recommended that she use ice and heat alternating on her back. I also recommended that she ambulate. If her symptoms do not improve or worsen, I want her to follow up with me over the next week.

