summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Rebecca Rodriguez is a 27-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of right knee pain.

The patient reports that she has been swimming more regularly over the course of the month. She has been trying to get up and get active. She has been doing breaststroke and has been feeling some pain in her right knee after swimming a couple of laps. She has been able to do 10 laps initially, but now she is only able to do a couple of laps before the pain starts. She denies any injury.

She reports that her back pain has been doing well. She has a history of depression and has been doing well on her current medication. She has been attending therapy and going outside more, which has been helping her. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations. She denies any pain going down her legs.

The patient has a history of carpal tunnel release done a couple of years ago. She reports that she has been getting full range of motion and it has felt better.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, weight loss, or weight gain.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies pain with leg movement.
• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.
• Psychiatric: Denies suicidal or homicidal ideations.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the knee: Full range of motion with pain with patella provocation. Examination of the lower extremities: Normal gait. No swelling in the joints. No pain with straight leg raise.
• Cardiovascular: Regular heartbeat.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Skin: No lymphadenopathy.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee is unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Rebecca Rodriguez is a 27-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of right knee pain.

Chondromalacia of the patella.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has a new onset of pain in her right knee after swimming. This is most likely due to overuse.
• Medical Treatment: I have recommended the patient to take ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food. She should avoid pressure on her knees.
• Additional Testing: She should continue to do straight leg raises to strengthen her quadriceps.
• Patient Education and Counseling: The patient should avoid lunges and breaststroke swimming. She can try other swimming strokes where her knees are straight or take a break.

Depression.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her depression.
• Medical Treatment: Continue current medication regimen.

Carpal tunnel syndrome.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her carpal tunnel syndrome.
• Medical Treatment: No changes at this time.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>
