summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

High blood pressure.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents for the management of hypertension. He reports that he was recently seen in the emergency department with high blood pressure and palpitations. He notes that he consumed several cups of coffee and had no breakfast on that day and believes that it was a panic attack. He denies recurrence since then. He has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension.

The patient reports that he was started on Benicar HCT 20/12.5 mg once daily and Amlodipine 10 mg once daily 1 month ago. He is compliant with his medications. He has been checking his blood pressure at home and reports that it has been running between 146 to 155/70-75 mmHg. He is currently taking his Benicar HCT 40/25 mg once daily and Amlodipine 10 mg once daily. He is not compliant with a low salt diet. He does try to exercise on a treadmill when he can.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Endorse history of hypertension.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle cramps.
• Neurological: Denies headaches.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Cardiovascular: Normal S1 and S2, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds. No jugular venous distention. No peripheral edema.

RESULTS

Thyroid profile from 03/01/2021 is available and reviewed today.

ASSESSMENT

• Hypertension.
• Palpitations.

PLAN

I discussed the patient's current diagnosis of hypertension with him today. I explained that his blood pressure has not improved on three antihypertensive medications. We discussed the risks associated with resistant hypertension, including the development of left ventricular hypertrophy. He was advised that his potassium level of 4.0 is within normal limits. I explained that there are two possible causes for his resistant hypertension. The first is that his body is producing an excess of aldosterone. The second is that his parathyroid gland is producing too much parathyroid hormone. I explained that the spironolactone can help to lower his aldosterone levels. He is currently taking hydrochlorothiazide, which can also cause his sodium levels to decrease. We discussed the risk of dehydration and electrolyte disturbances. He was advised to increase his potassium intake and to hydrate. I advised him to reduce his salt intake and to continue with a DASH diet. I provided him with a link to the American Heart Association's website, which has a lot of information about blood pressure and how to control it with diet and exercise. He was advised that if his blood pressure does not improve, the next step would be to send him to a resistant hypertension clinic.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will see him in 2 weeks to check his blood pressure and electrolytes. If his blood pressure does not improve on the addition of spironolactone, we will discuss the next steps which would be a renal denervation procedure.

HOSPITAL COURSE

The patient was seen in the emergency department with high blood pressure and palpitations. He was started on Benicar HCT 20/12.5 mg once daily and Amlodipine 10 mg once daily. He was advised to follow up with me in 1 month. He was compliant with his medications and returned today.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Family
Social
Personal
Allergies

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

Benicar HCT 40/25 mg once daily
Amlodipine 10 mg once daily

CURRENT VITALS

Blood pressure: 146 to 155/70-75 mmHg
Potassium: 4.0 mmol/L
Sodium: N/A
Thyroid: N/A

PHYSICIAN NOTES

The patient is a pleasant 49-year-old male who presents for the management of hypertension. He is compliant with his medications. His blood pressure is not controlled on three antihypertensive medications. He is currently taking hydrochlorothiazide and amlodipine. He is compliant with a low salt diet and exercises on a treadmill when he can. His blood pressure is elevated at home. His potassium level is 4.0 mmol/L. I discussed the risks associated with resistant hypertension and the possible causes for his elevated blood pressure. I advised him that we will continue to monitor his blood pressure and electrolytes and that if his blood pressure does not improve, the next step would be to send him to a resistant hypertension clinic. }}<|eot_id|>
