summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on 08/01/2020.

The patient was first seen in clinic in 05/2020 for complaints of 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. At that time, a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus was established with a spino catheter protocol. He underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was programmed at a level 2.0 was placed.

The patient reports that he has not been sick or hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He states that his walking has improved, as he is now able to walk to the dining room. However, he still has times where he needs help. He states that his bladder control improved after surgery, but has leveled out and he is now wearing diapers. He denies any sores from wearing the diaper or decreased or painful urination. He reports no headaches or pain where the shunt is. He denies any pain in his belly.

The patient's daughter reports that his walking has improved, he is now able to walk to the dining room, which he was not able to do before surgery. She notes that his bladder control did improve some after surgery, but now seems to have leveled out. She states that he is wearing diapers. She denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper or decreased or painful urination. She notes that he is able to have a normal conversation with her and that his sister, Hannah, stated "I feel like I can have a normal conversation with him again."

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Family
Social
Psychiatric
Surgical
Allergies
Immunizations

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.
Gastrointestinal: Denies pain with urination or sores.
Neurological: Denies headaches.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80.
Heart rate: 64.
Respirations: 18.
Weight: 174 pounds.
Pain: 0/10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Eyes: Cranial nerves intact. Pupils equal. EOMs intact. No nystagmus.
Neurological: Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination: Slow fine to nose. Of note, the shunt site was clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.

RESULTS

CT scan of the head 10/15/2020 showed a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus.
Gait impairment.
Urinary incontinence and urgency.
Cognitive impairment.

PLAN

At this time, I have recommended that we continue to leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months, as the patient is not experiencing any headaches or pain and has improved. The patient and family were advised that it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver safety evaluation. However, he could definitely pass it at that point and we could continue to talk about that. He could also enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
