- Examination of the skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV, no skin cancer. There are a few faint erythematous papules on bilateral medial cheeks and a small amount of erythema around and under her nostrils. There is no evidence of dryness.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Perioral dermatitis.
- Medical Reasoning: She has been using doxycycline once a day for a week now and has a small amount of residual rash. She has been using the sulfacet wash twice a day and the metro cream after washing her face. She has not been using any other lotions or creams. I recommend that she continues to use the wash twice a day. I also recommend that she use the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice a day until it is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. She can continue to use the face wash and metro cream.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that doxycycline is a low risk medication and that we are not going to have her on this forever. I recommended that she take it twice a day until the rash is gone, then once a day for a week before stopping. I also explained that the tacrolimus ointment may cause a tingling or needle-like sensation that should only last a few seconds and then go away. I advised her that if the rash starts to flair again, she can try using the tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If not, she can restart the doxycycline as well. She should contact me if the rash is getting worse rather than better. I also recommended that she keep using the sulfacet wash once a day because she did not notice any dryness and that it was feeling good. I also recommended that she keep using the metro cream. I also recommended that she should not use steroids on her face and that she should not use any other lotions or creams without consulting me.

2. Rosacea.
- Medical Reasoning: She has a history of rosacea and has been using doxycycline for a week.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that we will be treating her rosacea with the tacrolimus ointment. I recommended that she avoid using any anti-aging products or any products that contain plants.

3. Ocular rosacea.
- Medical Reasoning: She has a history of ocular rosacea.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I recommended that she gently wash her eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. She can also use a Bruder mask as needed.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue to use doxycycline 100 mg twice a day until the rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. Use the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice a day until it is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. She can continue to use the face wash and metro cream. If the rash starts to flair again, she can try using the tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If not, she can restart the doxycycline as well. She should contact me if the rash is getting worse rather than better. She should not use steroids on her face and should not use any other lotions or creams without consulting me. She should continue to use the sulfacet wash once a day because she did not notice any dryness and that it was feeling good. She can use a Bruder mask as needed.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
