summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENTING ILLNESS

The patient is a pleasant 40-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. She states that she hurt her left knee 1 week ago when she was running with her 5-year-old niece. She notes that she twisted her knee in an unusual way. She states she heard a pop at the time of the injury. She denies hearing a pop, but she notes that she has been limping since the injury. She reports her pain as a 4 out of 10 and states it has been throbbing. She has been taking Tylenol as needed and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She has been unable to take ibuprofen due to a recent ulcer. She states that Tylenol has been helping, but she can only take it every 4 hours.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has had an ulcer.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has a 5-year-old niece.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.
Skin: Reports history of ulcer.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema present. Effusion present. Moderate range of motion. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman's test. Negative valgus and varus test.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left knee was normal.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain, likely medial collateral ligament strain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have explained to her the diagnosis of a left knee medial collateral ligament strain. I have recommended that she take tramadol 50 mg twice daily and Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also recommended that she wear a knee immobilizer for the next week to reduce the pain and inflammation. She should ice her knee 3 to 4 times per day for 20 to 30 minutes. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks to allow for proper healing. The patient will follow up with me in 2 days if she feels her pain is getting worse. If she experiences any worsening of her pain, I recommended that she undergo further imaging such as an MRI.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 days. }}<|eot_id|>
